  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAn area of low pressure is currently seen on GOES satellite over \ncentral Illinois, moving eastward. Showers are seen tracking \neastward ahead of low pressure from Tennessee, Kentucky, and \nsouthwestern Ohio. Showers and a few thunderstorms will impact \nmainly northeast Kentucky, western West Virginia, and southeast Ohio \ntoday and tonight.\n\nAs low pressure slowly tracks east into Kentucky Sunday, \nareawide showers and thunderstorms are expected across the\nforecast area. If enough destabilization occurs during the day\nwith enough breaks in the clouds, a few strong thunderstorms \ncould develop. Some models indicate that this will occur with \n800-1,000 J/kg of MLCAPE developing by Sunday afternoon. The \nmain threat with any thunderstorms would be localized damaging \nwind gusts. SPC currently has a marginal risk of severe weather \noutlooked across the forecast area. \n\nFlooding will also be a concern with rounds of showers and \nstorms tracking southwest to northeast throughout the day. If \ntraining cells impact the same areas with repeated rounds of \nheavy rain, flooding could occur. The WPC currently has a \nmarginal risk of excessive rainfall across the forecast area for\nSunday. Rainfall amounts throughout the weekend are expected to\nbe about a half inch to an inch, with localized amounts up to\none and a half inches possible. The heaviest amounts will be\nacross the western half of the forecast area.\n\nThe threat of showers and thunderstorms will continue into the start \nof the new work week. Models indicate that the same low pressure \nsystem impacting the forecast area this weekend will be slow to \nexit, and an upper-level cutoff low may develop either Monday or \nTuesday somewhere in the Ohio Valley or Southeast. This will allow \nfor the continued development of showers and thunderstorms, \nespecially each afternoon and early evening during peak \nheating.",
